  "meta": {
    "title": "Squid Fall at 2000m: Chitin Rush of the Gulf of California",
    "date": "2026-09-20",
    "lesson": "A jumbo squid carcass sinks into the cold, dark mesopelagic and lands as a sudden pulse of chitin-rich food in an otherwise starved water column. Copiotrophic bacteria that carry strong chitinolytic enzymes rush to colonize the beak and mantle fragments, exploding in number within hours - but the same crowding that lets them feast also lets phage populations bloom explosively behind them, and the colonization collapses almost as fast as it began. This teaches boom-bust succession on marine snow falls: chitin degradation as the founding niche, density-dependent phage predation as the check on runaway growth, and the near-total darkness and cold of the deep water column as the backdrop that makes this pulse of carbon so valuable.",
    "citation": "Simon et al. 2002 Aquat Microb Ecol; De Leo et al. 2020 ISME J (Gulf of California squid falls)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Jumbo squid (Dosidicus gigas) carcass falls in the Gulf of California mesopelagic, chitinolytic Vibrio/Pseudoalteromonas colonization and phage-driven bloom collapse"
  },
